From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: glacier <hyouga.dev@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 11:06:15 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Trace rays on scalars instead of a Vec3D per step

World.rayTrace rebuilt the current point as a fresh Vec3D on every step of the DDA
walk - up to 200 per call - and the only things that read it were the next step's
arithmetic and Block.collisionRayTrace. Escape analysis could not remove them: the
object crossed a virtual call with six overrides, so the callee did not inline and
the allocation was never proved non-escaping.

Block.collisionRayTrace is now dispatched on a scalar signature and the six
overrides move with it; the Vec3D-taking form stays as a final bridge for anything
outside the walk. That lets the walk carry its point in three locals, and lets the
intersection test drop the two shift vectors and the six face candidates it built
per collidable block.

The arithmetic is transcribed, not rewritten - same face order, same strict "<" so
a tie still keeps the earlier face, and nz() reproduces the -0.0 folding that
Vec3D's constructor did at each point one was built. Checked against the original
algorithm over four million random box/ray pairs: identical results, -0.0 included.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

